  {
    "id": "interlaced",
    "title": "Interlaced",
    "description": [
      "The <i>Interlaced</i> series connects modern algorithmic design to its foundational lineage, the textile loom, which served as the structural precursor to early computing memory and binary systems. By utilizing low-resolution grids and strict sampling thresholds, the work isolates the underlying grammar of computation and allows it to dictate the textile's composition. Forcing fluid curves onto this low-resolution grid generates spatial friction. The resulting moiré patterns, pixelated checkerboards, and parabolic stars are the visual signatures of aliasing, the distortion that occurs when a machine resolves continuous math within a finite structure. ",
"Produced by hand on a vintage Passap E6000 knitting machine, the textiles rely on manual labor to realize digital logic. Individual pixel color values are transmitted to the machine as text strings of 0s, 1s, 2s, and 3s, requiring the carriage to be pulled across the needle bed line by line to render the composition."
],
